Yo for the right fax where we go to fetch session cookie, how safe is that?

For a backend-to-backend Spring Boot integration, the GET /API/login → rf-auth session cookie → reuse cookie pattern is reasonably safe, provided you treat that cookie essentially like a short-lived bearer credential.

Your RightFax design explicitly does:

GET /API/login
Authorization: Basic <credentials>
        ↓
200 OK
Set-Cookie: rf-auth=...
        ↓
reuse rf-auth on later calls
and the implementation brief already recommends holding it in memory and refreshing it on authentication failure rather than using a shared browser-style cookie store. 


The main security concern is credential/session theft, not the fact that it is a cookie.

The safe version
PANTS
  │
  │ HTTPS/TLS
  ▼
RightFax

1. Username/password retrieved from Secret Manager
2. GET /API/login over HTTPS
3. RightFax returns rf-auth
4. rf-auth kept only in application memory
5. Cookie sent only to RightFax
6. Cookie never logged
7. 401 → invalidate cookie → authenticate again
8. Credentials never appear in source/config repo
That is a normal enterprise service-to-service authentication model.

The unsafe version
HTTP instead of HTTPS                 ❌
log Authorization header              ❌
log rf-auth cookie                     ❌
store cookie in RocksDB                ❌
store cookie in Kafka message          ❌
hard-code password in application.yml ❌
disable TLS certificate validation     ❌
The important point is that HTTP Basic authentication itself does not encrypt anything. username:password is only Base64 encoded. So:

Basic Auth + HTTP  = bad
Basic Auth + HTTPS = acceptable
TLS provides the actual confidentiality.

How I would implement it
For your application, I like the design in the spec:

AtomicReference<AuthSession> authSession;
roughly:

Worker needs RightFax
      ↓
Do we have valid rf-auth?
    /             \
  yes              no
   |                |
use cookie       login once
                    |
                    v
               cache cookie
If four worker threads simultaneously discover that the session expired, don't let all four login independently:

Worker 1 ─┐
Worker 2 ─┤
Worker 3 ─┼──→ one synchronized refresh → new rf-auth
Worker 4 ─┘
That's exactly why your implementation brief proposes a single-flight refresh using a lock and rate-limited login attempts. 


I would not put rf-auth into RocksDB. After an application restart:

new JVM
   ↓
no cookie
   ↓
authenticate again
That's cleaner and safer.

One thing I'd ask RightFax
There are a few details your documents have not confirmed yet:

What is the rf-auth session lifetime and idle timeout?

That is already listed as an open item in your design. 


Also ask them:

Does rf-auth expire after fixed time or inactivity?

Is there an explicit logout endpoint?

Is the session invalidated when the password is rotated?

Can the same service account have multiple simultaneous sessions?

Is the cookie bound to a client/IP/session context?

Does production require HTTPS only?

Are there account lockout rules?

How I'd rate it
For your internal PANTS → on-prem RightFax setup:

HTTPS + service account + secret manager + in-memory rf-auth + no logging + controlled refresh = perfectly reasonable.

The cookie itself doesn't worry me much. I would be considerably more concerned if someone proposed putting the rf-auth value into Kafka/RocksDB or making the RightFax call over plain HTTP.


Ok so username password will co.e from edp vault, how to check tls thongy if tls is open on my linux from where i will call?

Yes. Think of this as three separate checks from the Linux VM where your Spring Boot service will run:

1. Can Linux reach the RightFax host/port?
2. Does that port actually speak TLS/HTTPS?
3. Does Linux/JVM trust the RightFax certificate?
TLS itself is not something you “open” on Linux like a service. Usually RightFax exposes HTTPS on something like 443, and your VM needs outbound network access to that host/port.

1. First check basic network connectivity
From the Linux server:

nc -vz rightfax-hostname 443
Example:

nc -vz rightfax-prod.company.com 443
Good:

Connection to rightfax-prod.company.com 443 port [tcp/https] succeeded!
Bad:

Connection timed out
That usually means firewall/routing/network access is missing.

You can also use:

telnet rightfax-prod.company.com 443
if nc is not installed.

2. Check the TLS handshake
This is the best command:

openssl s_client \
  -connect rightfax-prod.company.com:443 \
  -servername rightfax-prod.company.com
Near the bottom you want something like:

Protocol  : TLSv1.2
or:

TLSv1.3
and ideally:

Verify return code: 0 (ok)
That tells you:

Linux VM
   ↓
443 reachable
   ↓
TLS handshake successful
   ↓
certificate trusted
You can specifically test TLS 1.2:

openssl s_client \
  -connect rightfax-prod.company.com:443 \
  -servername rightfax-prod.company.com \
  -tls1_2
And TLS 1.3:

openssl s_client \
  -connect rightfax-prod.company.com:443 \
  -servername rightfax-prod.company.com \
  -tls1_3
If TLS 1.3 fails but TLS 1.2 works, that doesn't necessarily mean anything is wrong. Your RightFax environment may simply be configured for TLS 1.2.

3. Easiest practical test: curl
Do:

curl -v https://rightfax-prod.company.com/API/login
You don't even need to supply your real Vault credentials yet.

You're looking for output roughly like:

* Connected to rightfax-prod.company.com
* SSL connection using TLSv1.2
* Server certificate:
*  subject: ...
*  issuer: ...
*  SSL certificate verify ok.
Then you might receive:

HTTP/1.1 401 Unauthorized
And 401 is actually good for this test.

It means:

Network       ✅
TLS           ✅
RightFax API  ✅
Authentication ❌ because we intentionally didn't provide credentials
Once your Vault credentials are available, the actual flow becomes:

curl -v \
  -u "$RIGHTFAX_USER:$RIGHTFAX_PASSWORD" \
  https://rightfax-prod.company.com/API/login
But be careful doing that interactively because shell history/process inspection can expose credentials. For production, let the application retrieve them from your EDP Vault rather than manually putting passwords on command lines.

Very important: test Java trust too
Your OS trusting the certificate doesn't always guarantee that the JVM trusts it.

Since Spring Boot ultimately makes the HTTPS call through Java, check using:

keytool -printcert \
  -sslserver rightfax-prod.company.com:443
If Java can establish the connection and display the certificate chain, that's encouraging.

Your Java application may use:

JVM cacerts
rather than Linux's system CA store.

A classic failure you'll see in Spring Boot if the certificate isn't trusted is:

PKIX path building failed
or:

unable to find valid certification path to requested target
That means networking and TLS probably work, but Java doesn't trust the issuing CA.

For your RightFax setup, I would run these four commands
# DNS
nslookup rightfax-prod.company.com

# Network/firewall
nc -vz rightfax-prod.company.com 443

# TLS/certificate
openssl s_client \
  -connect rightfax-prod.company.com:443 \
  -servername rightfax-prod.company.com

# Actual HTTPS test
curl -v https://rightfax-prod.company.com/API/login
If you paste me the outputs of those four commands — redact hostnames/certificate details if needed — I can tell you exactly whether the problem, if any, is DNS, firewall, TLS, certificate trust, or RightFax itself.

And one thing I would not do as a solution is:

curl -k ...
or disabling certificate validation in WebClient. -k is okay for a quick diagnostic, but if it only works with -k, that means you have a certificate trust problem that should be fixed properly before production.

